// src/data/doctrineOfMean/doctrineOfMean2.js
const doctrineOfMean2 = [
  {
    id: 20,
    label: "20-1",
    text: {
      zh: `哀公問政。子曰：「文武之政，布在方策。其人存，則其政舉；其人亡，則其政息。人道敏政，地道敏樹。夫政也者，蒲盧也。故為政在人，取人以身，修身以道，修道以仁。仁者人也，親親為大；義者宜也，尊賢為大；親親之殺，尊賢之等，禮所生也。在下位不獲乎上，民不可得而治矣！故君子不可以不修身；思修身，不可以不事親；思事親，不可以不知人；思知人，不可以不知天。」`,
      en: `Duke Ai asked about government. The Master said, "The government of Wen and Wu is set out on wooden tablets and bamboo strips. While the right men are present, their government flourishes; when such men are gone, their government ceases. The way of man makes government spring up quickly, just as the way of earth makes trees grow quickly — government, indeed, is like a swift-growing reed. Therefore the conduct of government lies in getting the right men; to get the right men, one must cultivate one's own person; to cultivate one's person, one must follow the Way; to cultivate the Way, one must practice humaneness. Humaneness means being fully human, and loving one's kin is its greatest expression; rightness means doing what is fitting, and honoring the worthy is its greatest expression. The graduated love of kin and the ranked honoring of the worthy are what give rise to ritual propriety. If one in a subordinate position fails to win the confidence of those above, the common people cannot be governed. Therefore the noble person must not fail to cultivate his own person; wishing to cultivate his person, he must not fail to serve his parents; wishing to serve his parents, he must not fail to understand other men; wishing to understand other men, he must not fail to understand Heaven."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "魯哀公問政，孔子以文武之政作答，把「政」從典冊拉回到「人」身上。他先說：文武之政已布在方策之上，記載齊備；然而其人存，則其政舉；其人亡，則其政息——有合適的人在，政教便興起；人亡，政教便停息。典章再完備，沒有人去實行，也只是空文。人道敏政，地道敏樹；夫政也者，蒲盧也——為政像速生的蒲葦一樣，關鍵在得人，得人則政教敏速興起。故為政在人，取人以身，修身以道，修道以仁：得人靠自身修養，修身循道，修道以仁為本——一條鏈從「政」接到「人」，再接到「身」「道」「仁」。仁者人也，親親為大——仁就是把人當人，而以親愛親人為最大切入口；義者宜也，尊賢為大——義就是做得宜，而以尊敬賢人為最大切入口；親親之殺、尊賢之等，禮所生也——親愛親人有遠近差等，尊敬賢人有高下等級，禮便由此而生。在下位若不獲乎上，民不可得而治——下級得不到上級信任，百姓就治理不成。故君子不可不修身；思修身，不可不事親；思事親，不可不知人；思知人，不可不知天——由修身而事親、知人、知天，層層相扣，把為政的根本一路追到知天為止。",
      en: "Duke Ai asked about government, and Confucius answered from the government of Wen and Wu, pulling \"government\" back from documents onto \"persons.\" He first says: their policies are set out on wooden tablets and bamboo strips, fully recorded; yet while the right men are present, that government flourishes, and when such men are gone, it ceases — no matter how complete the statutes, without people to enact them they are empty text. The way of man makes government spring up quickly, as the way of earth makes trees grow quickly — government is like a swift-growing reed; the key is getting the right people, and with them government arises swiftly. Therefore the conduct of government lies in getting the right men; getting them depends on cultivating one's own person; cultivating the person follows the Way; cultivating the Way practices humaneness — a chain from government to persons, then to the person, the Way, and humaneness. Humaneness means being fully human, and loving one's kin is its greatest expression; rightness means doing what is fitting, and honoring the worthy is its greatest expression; the graduated love of kin and the ranked honoring of the worthy are what give rise to ritual. If one in a subordinate position fails to win the confidence of those above, the people cannot be governed. Therefore the noble person must cultivate his person; wishing to cultivate the person, he must serve his parents; wishing to serve his parents, he must understand other people; wishing to understand people, he must understand Heaven — a chain from self-cultivation through serving parents and knowing others to knowing Heaven, tracing the root of government all the way to knowing Heaven."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `想要把一件大事做好（無論是治理、管理或任何志業），先別急著設計制度或方法，而要先問：誰是對的人？而培養對的人（包括自己），要從最切身的孝親、待人做起，一步步扎根。
<br /><b>建議：今天想推動的某件事，先想清楚需要什麼樣的人來配合，並檢視自己是否已具備相應的修養。</b>`,
      en: `If you want to accomplish something significant — whether governing, managing, or pursuing any calling — don't rush to design systems or methods; first ask who the right people are. And cultivating the right people (including yourself) begins with the most immediate things: honoring your parents, treating others well, building outward step by step.
<br /><b>Suggestion: For something you want to accomplish today, first clarify what kind of people you need alongside you, and check whether you yourself already have the character it requires.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 21,
    label: "20-2",
    text: {
      zh: `天下之達道五，所以行之者三：曰君臣也，父子也，夫婦也，昆弟也，朋友之交也：五者天下之達道也。知、仁、勇三者，天下之達德也，所以行之者一也。或生而知之，或學而知之，或困而知之，及其知之一也；或安而行之，或利而行之，或勉強而行之，及其成功一也。子曰：「好學近乎知，力行近乎仁，知恥近乎勇。知斯三者，則知所以修身；知所以修身，則知所以治人；知所以治人，則知所以治天下國家矣。」`,
      en: `There are five universal paths for all under Heaven, and three virtues by which they are practiced: the relations between ruler and minister, father and son, husband and wife, elder and younger brother, and the association of friends — these five are the universal paths for all under Heaven. Wisdom, humaneness, and courage — these three are the universal virtues for all under Heaven, and the means by which they are practiced is one and the same. Some know it by birth, some know it through learning, some know it only through hardship — but once they know it, it is the same knowledge. Some practice it at ease, some practice it for its benefit, some practice it only through forced effort — but once they succeed, it is the same success. The Master said, "To love learning is close to wisdom; to practice earnestly is close to humaneness; to know shame is close to courage. To know these three is to know how to cultivate one's own person; to know how to cultivate one's own person is to know how to govern others; to know how to govern others is to know how to govern the states and all under Heaven."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章接著說明「達道」與「達德」，並由知、行兩路說到修身治國。天下之達道有五：君臣也，父子也，夫婦也，昆弟也，朋友之交也——君臣、父子、夫婦、兄弟、朋友這五倫，是天下人共通必經的道路，叫做達道。所以行之者三：知、仁、勇，是天下之達德；所以行之者一也——用以實行這五倫的根本是同一個（後文歸於誠）。無論知的途徑是生而知之、學而知之，還是困而知之，及其知之，一也——天生就懂、學了才懂、困頓後才懂，只要最終真懂，所知是一樣的；無論行的動力是安而行之、利而行之，還是勉強而行之，及其成功，一也——安然去做、因有利才做、勉強用力才做，只要最終做成，成功是一樣的。孔子又補上切近的入手：好學近乎知，力行近乎仁，知恥近乎勇——好學就接近智慧，努力實踐就接近仁，懂得羞恥就接近勇。知斯三者，則知所以修身；知所以修身，則知所以治人；知所以治人，則知所以治天下國家矣——由好學、力行、知恥切入修身，再由修身推到治人、治天下國家，層層展開。途徑可以不同，結果卻歸於一；切近的三「近乎」，正是普通人可走的門徑。整章把五倫、三德、知行層次與修齊治平連成一條可走的路。",
      en: "This chapter next explains the \"universal paths\" and \"universal virtues,\" and from the twin tracks of knowing and practicing moves on to self-cultivation and governing. There are five universal paths under Heaven: the relations of ruler and minister, father and son, husband and wife, elder and younger brother, and friends — paths everyone must travel, called the universal paths. They are practiced through three universal virtues — wisdom, humaneness, and courage — and the means of practice is one and the same (later gathered under sincerity). Whether one knows by birth, through learning, or only through hardship, once one knows, the knowledge is the same — born knowing, learning to know, or knowing only after difficulty, once truly known it is one knowledge; whether one practices at ease, for benefit, or only through forced effort, once one succeeds, the success is the same — practicing at ease, for advantage, or by forced effort, once achieved the success is one. Confucius adds the near approaches: to love learning is close to wisdom; to practice earnestly is close to humaneness; to know shame is close to courage. To know these three is to know how to cultivate one's person; knowing how to cultivate oneself is to know how to govern others; knowing how to govern others is to know how to govern the states and all under Heaven — from loving learning, earnest practice, and knowing shame into self-cultivation, then outward to governing people and the world. Paths may differ, yet the result is one; the three near approaches are the door ordinary people can walk through. The whole chapter joins the five relations, three virtues, levels of knowing and practicing, and the sequence from self-cultivation to ordering the world into one walkable path."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `不必因為自己不是「天生就懂」而氣餒——透過學習、透過在困境中摸索出來的道理，最終同樣是真知。同樣地，一開始靠意志力勉強自己去做對的事，也和天性使然一樣有價值，重點是持續下去。
<br /><b>建議：今天若有一件你「還做不好」的事，別因起步慢而放棄，持續努力就是進步的證明。</b>`,
      en: `Don't be discouraged just because you weren't "born knowing it" — understanding gained through study, or hard-won through difficulty, is genuine knowledge all the same. Likewise, forcing yourself through willpower to do the right thing at first is just as valuable as doing it naturally; what matters is persistence.
<br /><b>Suggestion: If there's something today you "still can't do well," don't give up just because you started slowly — continued effort is itself proof of progress.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 22,
    label: "20-3",
    text: {
      zh: `凡為天下國家有九經，曰：修身也，尊賢也，親親也，敬大臣也，體群臣也，子庶民也，來百工也，柔遠人也，懷諸侯也。修身則道立，尊賢則不惑，親親則諸父昆弟不怨，敬大臣則不眩，體群臣則士之報禮重，子庶民則百姓勸，來百工則財用足，柔遠人則四方歸之，懷諸侯則天下畏之。齊明盛服，非禮不動，所以修身也；去讒遠色，賤貨而貴德，所以勸賢也；尊其位，重其祿，同其好惡，所以勸親親也；官盛任使，所以勸大臣也；忠信重祿，所以勸士也；時使薄斂，所以勸百姓也；日省月試，既稟稱事，所以勸百工也；送往迎來，嘉善而矜不能，所以柔遠人也；繼絕世，舉廢國，治亂持危，朝聘以時，厚往而薄來，所以懷諸侯也。凡為天下國家有九經，所以行之者一也。`,
      en: `For governing the states of the world there are nine standing principles: cultivating one's own person, honoring the worthy, loving one's kin, respecting the great ministers, treating the body of officials as one's own, caring for the common people as one's children, attracting the various artisans, dealing kindly with people from afar, and cherishing the feudal lords. When one cultivates one's own person, the Way is established; when one honors the worthy, one is free from delusion; when one loves one's kin, uncles and brothers do not resent one; when one respects the great ministers, one is not confused in affairs; when one treats the officials as one's own body, their gratitude and service are profound; when one cares for the common people as one's children, the hundred surnames are encouraged to virtue; when one attracts the various artisans, wealth and resources are sufficient; when one deals kindly with people from afar, the four quarters turn toward one; when one cherishes the feudal lords, all under Heaven stands in awe. Fasting, purifying oneself, and dressing correctly, moving only in accordance with ritual propriety — this is the means of cultivating one's own person. Banishing flatterers and keeping seductive influences at a distance, making light of wealth and prizing virtue — this is the means of encouraging the worthy. Honoring their rank, enriching their emolument, sharing their likes and dislikes — this is the means of encouraging love of kin. Giving them a full staff of officers to employ as they see fit — this is the means of encouraging the great ministers. Dealing with them in good faith and granting generous pay — this is the means of encouraging the officers. Employing the people only at the proper seasons and taxing them lightly — this is the means of encouraging the common people. Inspecting them daily and examining them monthly, rewarding them according to their work — this is the means of encouraging the artisans. Welcoming them when they come and escorting them when they go, praising the capable among them and pitying the incapable — this is the means of dealing kindly with people from afar. Restoring extinguished lines of succession, reviving fallen states, bringing order to disorder and support to the endangered, receiving them in audience and sending envoys at the proper times, giving generously and receiving little — this is the means of cherishing the feudal lords. For governing the states of the world there are these nine standing principles, and the means by which they are practiced is one and the same.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章詳列「凡為天下國家有九經」——治理天下國家的九項常法，並先說功效、再說具體做法，最後歸到「所以行之者一也」。九經依次是：修身也，尊賢也，親親也，敬大臣也，體群臣也，子庶民也，來百工也，柔遠人也，懷諸侯也。先看九經各自帶來的功效：修身則道立——自身修好，治道才站得住；尊賢則不惑——尊敬賢人，臨事便不迷惑；親親則諸父昆弟不怨——親愛親族，伯父叔父與兄弟便無怨恨；敬大臣則不眩——敬重大臣，朝政便不昏亂眩惑；體群臣則士之報禮重——體恤群臣如體恤己身，士人回報的禮敬便厚重；子庶民則百姓勸——把庶民當子女般慈愛，百姓便互相勸勉向善；來百工則財用足——招來各方工匠，財用便充足；柔遠人則四方歸之——以柔和態度對待遠方之人，四方便來歸附；懷諸侯則天下畏之——安撫懷柔諸侯，天下便敬畏其德威。再逐項說明具體勸導與實行的做法。其一，所以修身也：齊明盛服，非禮不動——齋戒明潔、衣冠端正，不合禮的事不做。其二，所以勸賢也：去讒遠色，賤貨而貴德——除去讒言小人、疏遠美色誘惑，看輕財貨而看重德性，以此勸勉賢者。其三，所以勸親親也：尊其位，重其祿，同其好惡——尊重親族應有的位分，加重其俸祿，與之同好同惡，以此勸勉親愛親族。其四，所以勸大臣也：官盛任使——屬官充足、任其調遣使用，使大臣能展布，以此勸勉大臣。其五，所以勸士也：忠信重祿——以忠信待士，並給予厚祿，以此勸勉士人。其六，所以勸百姓也：時使薄斂——役使人民合乎時令、賦稅從輕，以此勸勉百姓。其七，所以勸百工也：日省月試，既稟稱事——每日省察、每月考核，發給的糧食俸給與事功相稱，以此勸勉百工。其八，所以柔遠人也：送往迎來，嘉善而矜不能——歡送離去者、迎接來者，嘉許有才能的、憐憫能力不足的，以此柔遠人。其九，所以懷諸侯也：繼絕世，舉廢國，治亂持危，朝聘以時，厚往而薄來——延續已絕的世系、振興已廢的國家，治理其亂、扶持其危，朝見聘問按時舉行，贈送豐厚而收受微薄，以此懷柔諸侯。文末重申：凡為天下國家有九經，所以行之者一也——項目雖有九，貫穿實行的根本卻只有一個（即後文之「誠」）。讀這一章，要把每一經的「功效」與「所以勸／所以行」的具體措施一一對上，才算把九經說全。",
      en: "This chapter sets out in detail \"for governing the states of the world there are nine standing principles,\" naming first each effect, then each concrete practice, and closing on \"the means by which they are practiced is one.\" The nine are, in order: cultivating oneself, honoring the worthy, loving kin, respecting great ministers, treating the body of officials as one's own, caring for the common people as children, attracting artisans, dealing kindly with people from afar, and cherishing the feudal lords. First the effects: cultivate the person and the Way is established — only when the self is cultivated does the Way of government stand; honor the worthy and one is free from delusion in affairs; love kin and uncles and brothers do not resent; respect great ministers and one is not confused or dazzled in court business; treat officials as one's own body and their return of ritual service is profound; care for the people as children and they encourage one another toward the good; attract artisans and resources suffice; deal kindly with those from afar and the four quarters turn toward one; cherish the lords and all under Heaven stands in awe of one's moral prestige. Then the concrete means, principle by principle. First, for cultivating oneself: fasting and purifying, dressing correctly, and moving only by ritual — do nothing that is not ritual. Second, for encouraging the worthy: banishing flatterers, keeping seduction distant, making light of wealth and prizing virtue. Third, for encouraging love of kin: honoring their rank, enriching their pay, sharing likes and dislikes. Fourth, for encouraging great ministers: giving them a full staff to employ so they can deploy their office. Fifth, for encouraging officers: good faith and generous pay. Sixth, for encouraging the people: seasonal labor and light taxes. Seventh, for encouraging artisans: daily inspection, monthly examination, and grain-and-pay matching the work. Eighth, for dealing kindly with people from afar: escorting those who leave and welcoming those who come, praising the able and pitying the unable. Ninth, for cherishing the lords: restoring extinguished lines, reviving fallen states, ordering disorder and supporting the endangered, receiving envoys in season, giving generously and receiving little. It closes by repeating: for governing the states there are these nine principles, and the means by which they are practiced is one and the same (the \"sincerity\" taken up later). To read this chapter fully is to match each principle's effect with its concrete \"encouragement\" or \"practice\" one by one."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `無論管理一個團隊或家庭，具體做法可以有很多面向——對人尊重、體諒辛勞、公平獎酬、善待新人——但貫穿其中的核心其實只有一個：真誠以待。與其死記每一條規則，不如先培養那份誠意，方法自然會跟著到位。
<br /><b>建議：今天在與團隊或家人互動時，先問自己是否出於真誠，再考慮具體做法是否恰當。</b>`,
      en: `Whether managing a team or a family, there are many concrete practices — respecting people, acknowledging their effort, rewarding fairly, welcoming newcomers — but running through all of them is really just one core thing: genuine sincerity. Rather than memorizing every rule, cultivate that sincerity first, and the right methods will follow naturally.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, when interacting with your team or family, first ask whether you're acting out of genuine sincerity, before worrying about whether the specific approach is right.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 23,
    label: "20-4",
    text: {
      zh: `凡事豫則立，不豫則廢。言前定則不跲，事前定則不困，行前定則不疚，道前定則不窮。在下位不獲乎上，民不可得而治矣；獲乎上有道：不信乎朋友，不獲乎上矣；信乎朋友有道：不順乎親，不信乎朋友矣；順乎親有道：反諸身不誠，不順乎親矣；誠身有道：不明乎善，不誠乎身矣。誠者，天之道也；誠之者，人之道也。誠者不勉而中，不思而得，從容中道，聖人也。誠之者，擇善而固執之者也。博學之，審問之，慎思之，明辨之，篤行之。有弗學，學之弗能弗措也；有弗問，問之弗知弗措也；有弗思，思之弗得弗措也；有弗辨，辨之弗明弗措也；有弗行，行之弗篤弗措也；人一能之己百之，人十能之己千之。果能此道矣，雖愚必明，雖柔必強。`,
      en: `In all things, preparation leads to success, and lack of preparation leads to failure. If what is to be said is determined beforehand, one will not stumble; if the affair is determined beforehand, one will not be at a loss; if one's conduct is determined beforehand, one will not be troubled; if the Way is determined beforehand, it will not be exhausted. If one in a subordinate position does not gain the confidence of those above, the common people cannot be governed. There is a way to gain the confidence of superiors: if one is not trusted by one's friends, one will not gain the confidence of superiors. There is a way to be trusted by one's friends: if one is not obedient to one's parents, one will not be trusted by one's friends. There is a way to be obedient to one's parents: if one examines oneself and finds oneself insincere, one will not be obedient to one's parents. There is a way to be sincere in oneself: if one does not understand what is truly good, one will not be sincere in oneself. Sincerity is the Way of Heaven; to become sincere is the way of humankind. One who is sincere hits upon what is right without effort, apprehends it without thinking, and naturally, effortlessly, is in accord with the Way — such a one is a sage. One who seeks to become sincere is one who chooses the good and holds fast to it. Study it extensively, inquire into it carefully, reflect on it deeply, sift it clearly, and practice it earnestly. When there is something not yet studied, or studied but not yet mastered, do not give up; when there is something not yet inquired into, or inquired into but not yet understood, do not give up; when there is something not yet reflected on, or reflected on but not yet grasped, do not give up; when there is something not yet sifted, or sifted but not yet made clear, do not give up; when there is something not yet practiced, or practiced but not yet made sincere, do not give up. If another succeeds by one effort, use a hundred efforts; if another succeeds by ten efforts, use a thousand. If one can truly follow this way, though dull, one is sure to become bright; though weak, one is sure to become strong.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章先立「凡事豫則立，不豫則廢」：凡事事先有準備則能成立，沒有準備則會廢敗。接著把「豫」展開為四個「前定」：言前定則不跲——話未出口先定準，說出便不絆跌失言；事前定則不困——事未做先定準，做時便不困窘無措；行前定則不疚——行動前先定準，做完便不內疚悔恨；道前定則不窮——行道前先定準，路上便不窮盡無門。有準備，言、事、行、道四路都不致失敗。接著鋪開一條由外而內、環環相扣的信任與誠身之鏈：在下位不獲乎上，民不可得而治矣——居下位而得不到上位者的信任，百姓就無法治理；獲乎上有道：不信乎朋友，不獲乎上矣——要獲上位者之信，先要見信於朋友，朋友都不信任你，上位者也不會信任你；信乎朋友有道：不順乎親，不信乎朋友矣——要見信於朋友，先要孝順父母，連父母都不順，朋友也不會信你；順乎親有道：反諸身不誠，不順乎親矣——要順乎親，先反觀自身是否真誠，自身不誠，便不能真正順親；誠身有道：不明乎善，不誠乎身矣——要自身真誠，先要明白何為善，不明善，誠便落不到實處。這一整條鏈從「治民」一路追到「明善」，說明外在信任的根在內在之誠與明善。然後點出全篇樞紐：誠者，天之道也；誠之者，人之道也——誠是天本來如此的道；努力去做到誠，則是人之道。誠者不勉而中，不思而得，從容中道，聖人也——天生至誠的人，不勉強也能中節，不刻意思索也能自得，從容合乎中道，這是聖人；誠之者，擇善而固執之者也——一般人求誠，便是選擇善而堅定實行、牢牢抓住。一般人的具體功夫是五步：博學之——廣泛學習；審問之——詳細詢問；慎思之——謹慎思考；明辨之——清楚辨析；篤行之——切實篤行。並且每一環都不許半途而廢：有弗學，學之弗能弗措也——除非不學，學了不到能，絕不放下；有弗問，問之弗知弗措也——除非不問，問了不到知，絕不放下；有弗思，思之弗得弗措也——除非不思，思了不到得，絕不放下；有弗辨，辨之弗明弗措也——除非不辨，辨了不到明，絕不放下；有弗行，行之弗篤弗措也——除非不行，行了不到篤，絕不放下。人一能之己百之，人十能之己千之——別人一次能做到，自己做一百次；別人十次能做到，自己做一千次。果能此道矣，雖愚必明，雖柔必強——真能按這條路走，即使愚笨也終必明達，即使柔弱也終必剛強。整章由「豫則立」接到誠身之鏈，再接到「誠／誠之」與博學審問慎思明辨篤行，終以「人一己百」保證愚柔亦可轉為明強。",
      en: "This chapter first establishes \"in all things, preparation leads to success; without preparation, failure\": with preparation beforehand things stand; without it they collapse. It then unfolds \"preparation\" into four prior determinations: if what is to be said is determined beforehand, one will not stumble in speech; if the affair is determined beforehand, one will not be at a loss; if conduct is determined beforehand, one will not be troubled with regret; if the Way is determined beforehand, it will not be exhausted — with preparation, speech, affairs, conduct, and the Way do not fail. It then lays out a tightly linked trust-and-sincerity chain from outer to inner: if one below fails to gain those above, the people cannot be governed; to gain those above there is a way — if one is not trusted by friends, one will not gain those above; to be trusted by friends there is a way — if one is not obedient to parents, one will not be trusted by friends; to be obedient to parents there is a way — if on examining oneself one is not sincere, one will not be obedient to parents; to be sincere in oneself there is a way — if one does not understand what is good, one will not be sincere in oneself. This whole chain traces governing the people back to understanding the good, showing that outer trust is rooted in inner sincerity and clarity about the good. It then names the pivot: sincerity is the Way of Heaven; becoming sincere is the way of humankind. One who is sincere hits the mark without effort, apprehends without thinking, and naturally accords with the Way — a sage; one who seeks to become sincere chooses the good and holds fast to it. Ordinary effort is five steps: study extensively, inquire carefully, reflect deeply, sift clearly, practice earnestly. And none of these may be abandoned halfway: unless one does not study, if one studies and has not yet mastered, do not give up; unless one does not inquire, if one inquires and has not yet understood, do not give up; unless one does not reflect, if one reflects and has not yet attained, do not give up; unless one does not sift, if one sifts and has not yet clarified, do not give up; unless one does not practice, if one practices and has not yet become earnest, do not give up. If another succeeds by one effort, use a hundred; if by ten, use a thousand. Truly follow this way, and though dull one becomes bright, though weak one becomes strong. The whole chapter moves from \"preparation leads to success\" into the sincerity chain, then into \"sincerity / becoming sincere\" and extensive study, careful inquiry, deep reflection, clear sifting, and earnest practice, ending with \"another's one, my hundred\" to guarantee that even the dull and weak can become bright and strong."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `「凡事豫則立」提醒我們，說話做事前先想清楚，可以省去許多後患。而「人一能之己百之」則是一劑最實際的勵志良藥——先天資質不足並非藉口，只要肯付出加倍的努力，一樣能達到目標。
<br /><b>建議：今天做一件重要的事之前，先花五分鐘想清楚步驟；若進度落後他人，就用加倍的努力補上，而非放棄。</b>`,
      en: `"Preparation leads to success" reminds us that thinking things through before speaking or acting can spare us much trouble later. "If another succeeds by one effort, use a hundred" is one of the most practical pieces of encouragement in the classics — a lack of natural talent is no excuse, since putting in redoubled effort can still get you there.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Before doing something important today, spend five minutes thinking through the steps; if you're behind others, make up for it with redoubled effort rather than giving up.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 24,
    label: "21",
    text: {
      zh: `自誠明，謂之性；自明誠，謂之教。誠則明矣，明則誠矣。`,
      en: `To become enlightened through sincerity is called nature; to become sincere through enlightenment is called instruction. Where there is sincerity, there is enlightenment; where there is enlightenment, there is sincerity.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章簡潔地指出兩條通往真理的路徑：一是「自誠明」，天生真誠自然而然通達明理，這是聖人的本性；二是「自明誠」，透過後天學習、明白道理，進而達到真誠，這是教育的作用。兩者其實殊途同歸——只要達到真誠，自然通達明理；只要通達明理，也必然趨於真誠。",
      en: "This brief chapter identifies two paths to the same truth: one is \"becoming enlightened through sincerity,\" where innate sincerity naturally opens into understanding — the way of the sage; the other is \"becoming sincere through enlightenment,\" where learning and understanding gradually lead one to sincerity — the work of education. In the end the two converge: to be sincere is to become clear-sighted, and to become clear-sighted is to arrive at sincerity."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `不是每個人都天生就通透明理，但透過持續學習、認識自己與世界，同樣可以走向真誠篤實。不必羨慕別人的天賦，你的成長路徑一樣能抵達同一個終點。
<br /><b>建議：今天透過閱讀或請教他人學到一個新的道理時，想想如何把它內化成自己真誠待人處事的一部分。</b>`,
      en: `Not everyone is born naturally perceptive, but through continued learning and coming to understand yourself and the world, you can arrive at the same sincerity and integrity. There's no need to envy others' natural gifts — your own path of growth can reach the very same destination.
<br /><b>Suggestion: When you learn something new today through reading or asking others, think about how to internalize it as part of how you sincerely treat people and handle matters.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 25,
    label: "22",
    text: {
      zh: `唯天下至誠，為能盡其性；能盡其性，則能盡人之性；能盡人之性，則能盡物之性；能盡物之性，則可以贊天地之化育；可以贊天地之化育，則可以與天地參矣。`,
      en: `Only the one who is utterly sincere in all under Heaven can fully realize his own nature. Being able to fully realize his own nature, he can fully realize the nature of other people. Being able to fully realize the nature of other people, he can fully realize the nature of all things. Being able to fully realize the nature of all things, he can assist in the transforming and nourishing work of Heaven and Earth. Being able to assist in the transforming and nourishing work of Heaven and Earth, he can take his place as a third alongside Heaven and Earth.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章描繪「至誠」所能達到的最高境界：一個人若能達到極致的真誠，就能充分實現自己的本性；能充分實現自己的本性，就能幫助他人充分實現各自的本性；進而能使萬物也充分實現其本性；如此便能協助天地化育萬物的偉大工作，最終達到與天地並列為三的崇高地位。這是儒家對「誠」的終極推崇——真誠不僅是個人修養，更是貫通己、人、物、天地的宇宙性力量。",
      en: "This chapter describes the highest state that \"utmost sincerity\" can reach: if a person attains complete sincerity, he can fully realize his own nature; fully realizing his own nature, he can help others fully realize theirs; and further, help all things fully realize their own natures. In doing so, he can assist Heaven and Earth in their great work of transformation and nourishment, ultimately attaining the exalted status of standing as a third alongside Heaven and Earth themselves. This is the Confucian tradition's highest praise of sincerity — not merely personal cultivation, but a cosmic force running through self, others, all things, and Heaven and Earth alike."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `真正認識並活出自己的本性，不只是為了自己好，更會自然而然地幫助身邊的人也活得更真實，甚至間接影響到你所接觸的環境與萬物。修養自己，其實是一件牽動整體的大事。
<br /><b>建議：今天花點時間誠實面對自己真正的天賦與渴望，並想想活出這份真實如何能同時利益他人。</b>`,
      en: `Truly knowing and living out your own nature isn't just for your own sake — it naturally helps the people around you live more authentically too, and even ripples outward to affect the environment and things you touch. Cultivating yourself is, in truth, a matter that moves the whole.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, take a moment to honestly face your true gifts and desires, and consider how living them out fully could also benefit others.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 26,
    label: "23",
    text: {
      zh: `其次致曲，曲能有誠，誠則形，形則著，著則明，明則動，動則變，變則化，唯天下至誠為能化。`,
      en: `Next comes the one who devotes himself to particular good points. From these particular points, sincerity can be attained. Once there is sincerity, it takes form; once it takes form, it becomes visible; once visible, it becomes manifest; once manifest, it moves others; once it moves others, it brings about change; once it brings about change, it transforms. Only the one who is utterly sincere in all under Heaven can transform others.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "並非人人都能像聖人一樣天生至誠、通貫一切；一般人可以從自己擅長或用心投入的某個小地方（曲）著手，日積月累，同樣能培養出真誠。真誠一旦具備，便會逐漸顯現於外、變得明顯、進而感動他人、帶動改變，最終產生深刻的轉化。這說明修養德行不必好高騖遠，從一件小事的真誠做起，同樣可以走向偉大的轉化力量。",
      en: "Not everyone possesses the sage's innate, all-encompassing sincerity from the start. Ordinary people can begin from some particular point — some small area where they apply themselves wholeheartedly — and over time cultivate genuine sincerity there too. Once sincerity takes hold, it gradually becomes visible, grows manifest, moves others, brings about change, and eventually produces profound transformation. This shows that cultivating virtue need not begin with lofty ambition; starting with sincerity in one small matter can still lead to a truly transformative power."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `不必等到自己「德行圓滿」才開始修養——選一件你目前真心投入、願意做到底的小事，把真誠貫徹在裡面，時間久了，它自然會顯現出感染他人的力量。
<br /><b>建議：今天挑一件你正在做的小事，全心全意、真誠地把它做到最好，不求立即的回報。</b>`,
      en: `You don't need to wait until you've achieved "complete virtue" to begin cultivating yourself — pick one small thing you're genuinely committed to right now, pour real sincerity into it, and over time it will naturally develop the power to move others.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, choose one small thing you're already working on, and do it with complete sincerity and your full effort, without expecting an immediate reward.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 27,
    label: "24",
    text: {
      zh: `至誠之道，可以前知。國家將興，必有禎祥；國家將亡，必有妖孽；見乎蓍龜，動乎四體。禍福將至：善，必先知之；不善，必先知之。故至誠如神。`,
      en: `The way of utmost sincerity enables one to know beforehand. When a state or a family is about to flourish, there are sure to be auspicious omens; when it is about to perish, there are sure to be ominous portents — these appear in the divination of milfoil and tortoise-shell, and stir within the movement of the four limbs. When calamity or blessing is about to arrive, the good is known beforehand, and the not-good is also known beforehand. Thus utmost sincerity is like a spirit.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章說明「至誠」具有一種近乎預知的能力：一個國家或家族將要興盛，必定會出現吉祥的徵兆；將要衰亡，也必定會出現不祥的徵兆，這些跡象會顯現在占卜或人的舉止之中。禍福將至之前，善或不善都能事先察覺。之所以能如此，是因為極致的真誠使人心澄澈敏銳，能洞察事物發展的細微徵兆，就像神明一樣靈驗。",
      en: "This chapter explains that \"utmost sincerity\" carries an almost prophetic capacity: when a state or family is about to flourish, auspicious signs are sure to appear; when it is about to decline, ominous signs are sure to appear — visible in divination or in the conduct of those involved. Before fortune or misfortune arrives, whether it will be good or ill can be sensed in advance. This is possible because utmost sincerity clarifies and sharpens the mind, enabling one to perceive the subtle early signs of how things are developing — as unerring as a spirit."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `與其迷信外在的占卜或徵兆，不如培養內心真誠、澄澈的覺察力——當你對自己與周遭夠誠實、夠敏銳時，往往能提早察覺一件事情正在往好或壞的方向發展，及早因應。
<br /><b>建議：今天留意生活或工作中一個微小的異常訊號，誠實地面對它，而不是視而不見。</b>`,
      en: `Rather than relying on external omens or divination, cultivate an inner sincerity and clarity of awareness — when you're honest and attentive enough toward yourself and your surroundings, you can often sense early on whether something is heading toward good or bad, and respond in time.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, notice one small unusual signal in your life or work, and face it honestly instead of looking away.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 28,
    label: "25",
    text: {
      zh: `誠者自成也，而道自道也。誠者物之終始，不誠無物。是故君子誠之為貴。誠者非自成己而已也，所以成物也。成己，仁也；成物，知也。性之德也，合外內之道也，故時措之宜也。`,
      en: `Sincerity is self-completing, and the Way is self-directing. Sincerity is the end and the beginning of all things; without sincerity there would be nothing. Therefore the noble person prizes sincerity above all. Sincerity is not merely the completion of oneself — it is also the means of completing other things. Completing oneself is humaneness; completing other things is wisdom. These are virtues of one's nature, the way that unites the outer and the inner; therefore, whenever applied, sincerity is always fitting.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `本章指出「誠者自成也，而道自道也」——「誠」不是外在強加的規範，而是自我完成、自然而然的道理；道也是自行其道、不待外力強推。「誠者物之終始，不誠無物」——誠是萬事萬物得以存在、從始到終一貫的根本；若沒有真誠，事物便沒有真正的實質可言。是故君子誠之為貴——因此君子最重視真誠。「誠者非自成己而已也，所以成物也」——真誠不僅是成就自己，也必然延伸到成就他人與萬物。成己，仁也；成物，知也——成就自己是仁，成就外物是智。性之德也，合外內之道也——這是內在本性的德行，能同時貫通內外；故時措之宜也——所以無論運用在何處、何時措用，都能恰到好處。整章把誠從「自成」推到「成物」，再落到仁與知、外內合一、時措皆宜，說明誠既是成己的根，也是成物的橋。`,
      en: `This chapter opens with "sincerity is self-completing, and the Way is self-directing" — sincerity is not an externally imposed rule but something that completes itself of its own nature; the Way likewise directs itself without being forced from outside. "Sincerity is the end and the beginning of all things; without sincerity there would be nothing" — sincerity is the root by which all things come to exist and remain consistent from start to finish; without it there is no true substance to anything. Therefore the noble person prizes sincerity above all. "Sincerity is not merely the completion of oneself — it is also the means of completing other things." Completing oneself is humaneness; completing other things is wisdom. These are virtues of one's nature, the way that unites the outer and the inner; therefore, whenever applied, sincerity is always fitting. The whole chapter moves sincerity from self-completion to completing things, then lands on humaneness and wisdom, the union of outer and inner, and fitting application at every time — sincerity is both the root of completing oneself and the bridge to completing others.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `做任何事若少了真誠，即使表面完成了，也稱不上真正做到；而真誠一旦具備，不只成就了自己，也自然會延伸出去，幫助身邊的人與事。誠意，是所有努力的地基。
<br /><b>建議：今天檢視手邊一項正在做的事，問問自己：這件事我是真心投入，還是只是應付了事？</b>`,
      en: `Anything done without sincerity, even if it appears finished on the surface, is not truly accomplished; but once sincerity is present, it not only fulfills yourself but naturally extends outward to help the people and matters around you. Sincerity is the foundation beneath every effort.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, examine something you're currently working on and ask yourself: am I genuinely invested in this, or just going through the motions?</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 29,
    label: "26-1",
    text: {
      zh: `故至誠無息。不息則久，久則徵，徵則悠遠，悠遠則博厚，博厚則高明。博厚，所以載物也；高明，所以覆物也；悠久，所以成物也。博厚配地，高明配天，悠久無疆。如此者，不見而章，不動而變，無為而成。`,
      en: `Therefore utmost sincerity never ceases. Not ceasing, it endures; enduring, it gives evidence of itself; giving evidence of itself, it reaches far and long; reaching far and long, it becomes broad and deep; being broad and deep, it becomes high and brilliant. Being broad and deep is the means by which it supports all things; being high and brilliant is the means by which it covers all things; reaching far and enduring long is the means by which it brings all things to completion. In breadth and depth it matches Earth; in height and brilliance it matches Heaven; in reaching far and enduring long it is without limit. Being such, without being seen it becomes manifest, without moving it brings about change, and without acting it achieves completion.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章描述「至誠」的持續力量：真正的真誠是不會停止的，正因為不間斷，才能長久；長久才能顯現出實際的成效；有了成效才能達到深遠廣大、進而崇高光明的境界。廣博深厚是用來承載萬物，崇高光明是用來覆蓋萬物，悠遠長久則是用來成就萬物。這樣的至誠，廣厚可與大地相配，崇高光明可與上天相配，悠久無窮則沒有極限——不需刻意顯露就自然彰顯，不需刻意行動就能引發改變，不需勉強作為就能有所成就。",
      en: "This chapter describes the enduring power of \"utmost sincerity\": true sincerity never stops, and precisely because it does not cease, it endures long; enduring long, it produces visible results; producing results, it reaches a state of vast depth and, further, of lofty brilliance. Its breadth and depth allow it to support all things; its loftiness and brilliance allow it to cover all things; its far-reaching endurance allows it to bring all things to completion. Such utmost sincerity, in its breadth and depth, matches the Earth; in its loftiness and brilliance, matches Heaven; in its far-reaching endurance, is without limit — manifesting itself without needing to be displayed, bringing about change without needing to act, achieving completion without needing to strive."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `真正持久有效的影響力，不是靠一時的表現或刻意造勢，而是靠不間斷的真誠累積出來的——就像大地默默承載萬物、上天默默覆蓋萬物，時間久了，自然會顯現出深遠的力量。
<br /><b>建議：今天別急著證明自己或刻意表現，而是選擇一件值得長期堅持的事，安靜地持續做下去。</b>`,
      en: `Truly lasting and effective influence isn't built through momentary displays or deliberate self-promotion — it accumulates from uninterrupted sincerity, the way the earth silently supports all things and heaven silently covers them. Given enough time, that quiet consistency naturally reveals a far-reaching power.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, instead of rushing to prove yourself or put on a show, pick one thing worth sustaining over the long term, and quietly keep doing it.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 30,
    label: "26-2",
    text: {
      zh: `天地之道，可一言而盡也：其為物不貳，則其生物不測。天地之道：博也，厚也，高也，明也，悠也，久也。今夫天，斯昭昭之多，及其無窮也，日月星辰繫焉，萬物覆焉。今夫地，一撮土之多，及其廣厚，載華嶽而不重，振河海而不洩，萬物載焉。今夫山，一卷石之多，及其廣大，草木生之，禽獸居之，寶藏興焉。今夫水，一勺之多，及其不測，黿鼉、蛟龍、魚鱉生焉，貨財殖焉。《詩》云：「維天之命，於穆不已！」蓋曰天之所以為天也。「於乎不顯！文王之德之純！」蓋曰文王之所以為文也，純亦不已。`,
      en: `The way of Heaven and Earth can be summed up in a single phrase: since it is undivided in its being, its production of things is unfathomable. The way of Heaven and Earth is broad, is deep, is high, is bright, is far-reaching, is enduring. Now this heaven is but a mass of glimmering points of light, yet in its infinite extent the sun, moon, stars, and constellations are suspended in it, and all things are covered by it. Now this earth is but a handful of soil, yet in its breadth and depth it supports Mount Hua without feeling its weight, and gathers in the rivers and seas without letting them leak away, and all things are supported by it. Now this mountain is but a mass of small stones, yet in its vastness grasses and trees grow upon it, birds and beasts dwell on it, and hidden treasures are found within it. Now this water is but a single ladleful, yet in its unfathomable depths the giant turtle, the crocodile, the dragon, fish, and soft-shelled turtles are produced in it, and wealth and resources multiply within it. The Book of Odes says, "The mandate of Heaven, how deeply solemn and unceasing!" — this means that this is what makes Heaven to be Heaven. "Ah, how illustrious, the purity of King Wen's virtue!" — this means that this is what made King Wen to be what he was, a purity likewise unceasing.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章承接「至誠無息」，用天地山水之喻說明「其為物不貳，則其生物不測」，並以文王之德之純作結。天地之道，可一言而盡也：其為物不貳，則其生物不測——天地之為物專一不貳，所以化生萬物的功能深不可測。天地之道的氣象是：博也，厚也，高也，明也，悠也，久也——廣博、深厚、高大、光明、悠遠、長久。經文接著用四組「今夫……及其……」說明偉大由微小純一積累而成。今夫天，斯昭昭之多——起初不過這一點點昭昭微光，及其無窮也，日月星辰繫焉，萬物覆焉——推到無窮，日月星辰都懸繫其上，萬物都覆庇其下。今夫地，一撮土之多——起初不過一撮土，及其廣厚，載華嶽而不重，振河海而不洩，萬物載焉——推到廣厚，承載華山也不覺得重，收納河海也不洩漏，萬物都托載其上。今夫山，一卷石之多——起初不過一堆小石，及其廣大，草木生之，禽獸居之，寶藏興焉——推到廣大，草木生長、禽獸棲居、寶藏興起。今夫水，一勺之多——起初不過一勺水，及其不測，黿鼉、蛟龍、魚鱉生焉，貨財殖焉——推到深不可測，黿鼉蛟龍魚鱉都在其中生育，貨財也在其中繁殖。偉大皆由微小、純一、不貳的累積而成，正如至誠無息之德。最後引《詩》「維天之命，於穆不已」——蓋曰天之所以為天也，在於天命深遠而不已；「於乎不顯！文王之德之純」——蓋曰文王之所以為文也，亦在德之純而不已。純亦不已，正與至誠無息相應：天以不已為天，文以純不已為文。",
      en: "This chapter continues \"utmost sincerity never ceases,\" using Heaven, Earth, mountains, and water to show that \"since it is undivided in its being, its production of things is unfathomable,\" and closes on the purity of King Wen's virtue. The way of Heaven and Earth can be summed up in one phrase: undivided in what it is, unfathomable in what it produces — because Heaven and Earth are single and undivided as things, their power to generate things is unfathomably deep. The character of that way is: broad, deep, high, bright, far-reaching, and enduring. The text then uses four \"now as for... and when it reaches...\" sequences to show that greatness accumulates from the small and pure. Now as for Heaven: it begins as but a mass of glimmering points, yet when it reaches infinity, sun, moon, stars, and constellations hang in it and all things are covered. Now as for Earth: it begins as but a handful of soil, yet when it reaches breadth and depth it supports Mount Hua without feeling the weight, holds rivers and seas without leaking, and carries all things. Now as for a mountain: it begins as but a mass of small stones, yet when it reaches vastness grasses and trees grow, birds and beasts dwell, and treasures arise. Now as for water: it begins as but a ladleful, yet when it reaches unfathomable depths turtles, crocodiles, dragons, fish, and soft-shelled turtles are born, and wealth multiplies. Greatness comes from small, pure, undivided accumulation — just like the virtue of utmost sincerity that never ceases. Finally the Odes are cited: \"The mandate of Heaven, how deeply solemn and unceasing!\" — this is what makes Heaven Heaven, a mandate deep and unceasing; \"Ah, how illustrious, the purity of King Wen's virtue!\" — this is what made Wen who he was, a purity likewise unceasing. Pure and unceasing matches utmost sincerity that never stops: Heaven is Heaven by never ceasing; Wen is Wen by purity that never ceases."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `偉大的事物往往不是憑空而來，而是由微小卻不間斷的累積構成——一點光累積成日月星辰，一撮土累積成承載萬物的大地。你今天看似微不足道的努力，只要持續純一不間斷，終將累積成可觀的成果。
<br /><b>建議：今天做一件小小的、看似不起眼的努力，並提醒自己：只要持續不斷，它終將累積成重要的東西。</b>`,
      en: `Great things rarely appear out of nowhere — they're built from small but unceasing accumulation, just as points of light accumulate into the sun, moon, and stars, and handfuls of soil accumulate into an earth that supports all things. Whatever small effort you make today, if sustained purely and without interruption, will eventually accumulate into something significant.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, make one small, seemingly insignificant effort, and remind yourself: if kept up without interruption, it will eventually accumulate into something important.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 31,
    label: "27",
    text: {
      zh: `大哉聖人之道！洋洋乎！發育萬物，峻極於天。優優大哉！禮儀三百，威儀三千。待其人而後行。故曰苟不至德，至道不凝焉。故君子尊德性而道問學，致廣大而盡精微，極高明而道中庸。溫故而知新，敦厚以崇禮。是故居上不驕，為下不倍，國有道其言足以興，國無道其默足以容。《詩》曰「既明且哲，以保其身」，其此之謂與！`,
      en: `How great is the way of the sage! Overflowing, it gives birth and growth to all things, rising to the height of Heaven. How abundant and great it is! Three hundred rules of ceremony, three thousand rules of conduct — it awaits the right person before it can be put into practice. Hence it is said: unless there is perfect virtue, the perfect Way cannot be realized. Therefore the noble person honors his moral nature and pursues inquiry and study, extending breadth and greatness while exhausting the subtle and minute, reaching the utmost height and brilliance while following the path of the mean. He reviews the old and comes to know the new; he deepens his sincerity and honors ritual propriety. Thus, in a high position he is not arrogant, and in a low position he is not rebellious. When the state has the Way, his words are sufficient to raise it up; when the state lacks the Way, his silence is sufficient to preserve him. The Book of Odes says, "Being wise and understanding, he thereby preserves his own person" — is this not what is meant?`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章以「大哉聖人之道」起興，把聖道的盛大落到君子雙軌並進的修養與進退之智。聖人之道洋洋乎！發育萬物，峻極於天——浩浩蕩蕩，化育萬物，高峻上達於天。優優大哉！禮儀三百，威儀三千——充裕盛大，大禮儀節有三百，小節威儀有三千，待其人而後行——必須等到有德之人出現，這些禮才能真正實行。故曰苟不至德，至道不凝焉——沒有至德，至道便凝聚不成、落不到實處。因此君子的修養是雙軌並行：尊德性而道問學——既尊崇固有德性，又走問學之路；致廣大而盡精微——既推到廣大之境，又窮盡精微之處；極高明而道中庸——既臻於高明，又不離中庸平常之路。溫故而知新，敦厚以崇禮——溫習已知以悟新知，敦篤厚實以推崇禮。是故居上不驕，為下不倍——在上位不驕傲，在下位不背叛；國有道，其言足以興——國家有道時，他的言論足以振興；國無道，其默足以容——國家無道時，他的沉默足以容身自保。最後引《詩》「既明且哲，以保其身」，正是這種既能有為、又能自保的明智。整章把聖道的盛大，落到君子如何同時顧及德性與問學、廣大與精微、高明與中庸，並在有道無道之時進退得宜。",
      en: "This chapter opens with \"How great is the way of the sage!\" and lands that grandeur on the noble person's twin-track cultivation and wisdom of advance and withdrawal. The sage's way is overflowing — it gives birth and growth to all things, rising to the height of Heaven. Abundant and great — with three hundred rules of ceremony and three thousand rules of conduct — it awaits the right person before it can be practiced. Hence: unless there is perfect virtue, the perfect Way cannot be realized or made to cohere. Therefore the noble person's cultivation runs on twin tracks: he honors his moral nature and pursues inquiry and study; he extends breadth and greatness while exhausting the subtle and minute; he reaches the utmost height and brilliance while following the path of the mean. He reviews the old and comes to know the new; he deepens sincerity and honors ritual. Thus in a high position he is not arrogant, and in a low position he is not rebellious; when the state has the Way, his words suffice to raise it up; when the state lacks the Way, his silence suffices to preserve him. The Odes' \"Being wise and understanding, he thereby preserves his own person\" names exactly this intelligence that can both act and protect itself. The whole chapter lands the grandeur of the sage's way on how the noble person holds together moral nature and learning, breadth and subtlety, loftiness and the mean, and advances or withdraws fittingly when the state has or lacks the Way."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `修養自己不必在「胸懷大志」與「注重細節」之間二選一，也不必在「堅守原則」與「靈活應變」之間對立——真正成熟的態度是兩端都顧到：既有大格局，也不忽略小事；既溫故，也知新。
<br /><b>建議：今天處理一件事時，同時檢視大方向是否正確，以及細節是否到位，兩者都不偏廢。</b>`,
      en: `Cultivating yourself doesn't require choosing between "having grand ambitions" and "attending to details," nor between "holding firm principles" and "adapting flexibly" — true maturity holds both ends together: having the big picture while not neglecting small things, reviewing what's old while also grasping what's new.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, when handling something, check both whether the overall direction is right and whether the details are in place — don't neglect either.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 32,
    label: "28",
    text: {
      zh: `子曰：「愚而好自用，賤而好自專，生乎今之世，反古之道。如此者，菑及其身者也。」非天子，不議禮，不制度，不考文。今天下車同軌，書同文，行同倫。雖有其位，苟無其德，不敢作禮樂焉；雖有其德，苟無其位，亦不敢作禮樂焉。子曰：「吾說夏禮，杞不足徵也；吾學殷禮，有宋存焉；吾學周禮，今用之，吾從周。」`,
      en: `The Master said, "To be ignorant yet fond of relying on one's own judgment; to be lowly yet fond of monopolizing authority; to live in the present age yet turn back to the ways of antiquity — one who does such things brings calamity upon himself." Unless one is the Son of Heaven, one does not deliberate on rites, does not fix standards and measures, does not examine and settle written characters. Now, throughout the world, carriages have wheels of the same gauge, writing employs the same characters, and conduct follows the same moral order. Though one may hold the position, if one lacks the corresponding virtue, one dares not create rites and music; though one may possess the virtue, if one lacks the corresponding position, one likewise dares not create rites and music. The Master said, "I can describe the rites of the Xia dynasty, but the state of Qi cannot sufficiently confirm them; I have learned the rites of the Yin dynasty, and they are preserved in the state of Song; I have learned the rites of the Zhou dynasty, which are used at present — I follow the Zhou."`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `子曰：「愚而好自用，賤而好自專，生乎今之世，反古之道。如此者，菑及其身者也。」——愚昧卻喜歡自作主張，地位卑微卻喜歡獨攬大權，生在當今卻一味想恢復古代的制度，這樣的人只會招致災禍臨身。非天子，不議禮，不制度，不考文——除非是天子，否則不能擅自議定禮制、制定法度、考訂文字。今天下車同軌，書同文，行同倫——如今天下車輛的軌距一致、文字書寫一致、倫理行為的準則也一致，說明統一的秩序已成當世的實際。雖有其位，苟無其德，不敢作禮樂焉；雖有其德，苟無其位，亦不敢作禮樂焉——即使有相應的地位，若沒有相應的德行，不敢制作禮樂；即使有相應的德行，若沒有相應的地位，也不敢制作禮樂。位與德必須相稱，才能動禮樂。子曰：「吾說夏禮，杞不足徵也；吾學殷禮，有宋存焉；吾學周禮，今用之，吾從周。」——孔子說他能講述夏禮，但杞國的文獻不足以充分印證；他學過殷禮，宋國還保存著一些；他學過周禮，當今正在使用，所以他遵從周禮。整章把「愚而自用、賤而自專、生今反古」的災禍，對到「非天子不議禮制度考文」與「有位無德、有德無位皆不敢作禮樂」，再落到孔子「吾從周」的現實選擇：禮制既不可妄作，也不可一味復古，而要與德、位、當世之用相合。`,
      en: `The Master said, "To be ignorant yet fond of relying on one's own judgment; to be lowly yet fond of monopolizing authority; to live in the present age yet turn back to the ways of antiquity — one who does such things brings calamity upon himself." Unless one is the Son of Heaven, one does not deliberate on rites, does not fix standards and measures, does not examine and settle written characters. Now, throughout the world, carriages have wheels of the same gauge, writing employs the same characters, and conduct follows the same moral order — a unified order is already the fact of the age. Though one may hold the position, if one lacks the corresponding virtue, one dares not create rites and music; though one may possess the virtue, if one lacks the corresponding position, one likewise dares not create them. Position and virtue must match before rites and music may be remade. The Master said, "I can describe the rites of the Xia, but the state of Qi cannot sufficiently confirm them; I have learned the rites of the Yin, and they are preserved in Song; I have learned the rites of the Zhou, which are used at present — I follow the Zhou." The whole chapter maps the calamity of ignorant self-reliance, lowly monopoly of power, and living now while reversing to antiquity onto "unless Son of Heaven, do not deliberate rites, set standards, or fix writing," and onto "with position but no virtue, or virtue but no position, one dares not create rites and music," then lands on Confucius's realistic choice — "I follow Zhou": rites must neither be made rashly nor restored blindly, but fitted to virtue, position, and present use.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `提出改變或創新之前，要先誠實評估自己是否具備相應的能力與位置——光有理想卻無實力或無立場，貿然行動只會壞事；反之，若條件具足卻墨守成規、堅持過時的老方法，也是一種偏執。找到「合宜的時機與位置」再行動，才是智慧。
<br /><b>建議：今天若有想推動的改變，先評估自己目前是否具備足夠的位置與能力，再決定是否及如何行動。</b>`,
      en: `Before proposing change or innovation, honestly assess whether you actually have the ability and standing to back it up — having ideals without the capacity or position to match, and acting rashly anyway, only makes things worse; conversely, clinging to outdated methods when you do have the means to improve is its own kind of stubbornness. Finding the right timing and position before acting is real wisdom.
<br /><b>Suggestion: If there's a change you want to push for today, first assess whether you currently have the standing and capability to back it, then decide whether and how to act.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 33,
    label: "29",
    text: {
      zh: `王天下有三重焉，其寡過矣乎！上焉者雖善無徵，無徵不信，不信民弗從；下焉者雖善不尊，不尊不信，不信民弗從。故君子之道：本諸身，徵諸庶民，考諸三王而不繆，建諸天地而不悖，質諸鬼神而無疑，百世以俟聖人而不惑。質諸鬼神而無疑，知天也；百世以俟聖人而不惑，知人也。是故君子動而世為天下道，行而世為天下法，言而世為天下則。遠之則有望，近之則不厭。《詩》曰：「在彼無惡，在此無射；庶幾夙夜，以永終譽！」君子未有不如此而蚤有譽於天下者也。`,
      en: `To rule all under Heaven there are three important matters, by attending to which one may reduce one's errors! What was excellent in ages of old cannot be verified; being unverified, it inspires no trust; inspiring no trust, the people will not follow it. What is excellent in the present age, if it does not carry proper standing, likewise inspires no trust; inspiring no trust, the people will not follow it. Therefore the way of the noble person is rooted in his own person, verified among the common people, examined against the practice of the Three Kings and found without error, established before Heaven and Earth and found without contradiction, submitted to the spirits and ghosts without doubt, and able to await a sage a hundred generations hence without perplexity. That it can be submitted to the spirits and ghosts without doubt shows that he knows Heaven; that it can await a sage a hundred generations hence without perplexity shows that he knows humanity. Therefore, when the noble person moves, he becomes for generations the Way of all under Heaven; when he acts, he becomes for generations the model of all under Heaven; when he speaks, he becomes for generations the standard of all under Heaven. Those far from him look up to him with longing; those close to him never tire of him. The Book of Odes says, "There, he is disliked by none; here, none grow weary of him; from dawn to night he strives, that his praise may endure forever." Never has there been a noble person who did not act in this way, yet who early won praise throughout all under Heaven.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章論「王天下有三重焉，其寡過矣乎」——統治天下有三件重大之事，能留意便可少過。經文先從「信」與「從」說起：上焉者雖善無徵，無徵不信，不信民弗從——前代的做法雖善，若無可徵驗的實據，便不能取信，不能取信則民不跟從；下焉者雖善不尊，不尊不信，不信民弗從——當下在下位者的做法雖善，若無應有的尊位，同樣不能取信，民也不跟從。善而無人信、無人從，便不能王天下。故君子之道必須層層可證：本諸身——以自身為根本；徵諸庶民——在百姓那裡得到徵驗；考諸三王而不繆——考察夏商周三代聖王而不謬誤；建諸天地而不悖——建立於天地之間而不違背；質諸鬼神而無疑——質正於鬼神而無所疑；百世以俟聖人而不惑——等到百世之後的聖人來檢驗也不迷惑。質諸鬼神而無疑，是知天；百世以俟聖人而不惑，是知人——知天知人，才算把道站穩。是故君子動而世為天下道，行而世為天下法，言而世為天下則——一舉一動、所行所言，世代都成為天下的道路、法度與準則；遠之則有望，近之則不厭——遠處的人仰望他，近處的人也不厭棄他。引《詩》「在彼無惡，在此無射；庶幾夙夜，以永終譽」——在那邊無人憎惡，在這邊無人厭煩；幾乎從早到晚努力，以使榮譽長久。說明君子未有不如此而蚤有譽於天下者——從來沒有人不如此做卻能很早便名揚天下的。整章把可信之道，落在自身、庶民、三王、天地、鬼神與百世聖人的層層檢驗上。",
      en: "This chapter treats \"to rule all under Heaven there are three important matters, by attending to which one may reduce one's errors.\" It begins from trust and following: what was excellent in ages of old cannot be verified — unverified, it inspires no trust, and without trust the people will not follow; what is excellent now among those below, if it lacks proper standing, likewise inspires no trust, and the people will not follow. Excellence that no one trusts or follows cannot rule the world. Therefore the way of the noble person must be verifiable layer by layer: rooted in his own person; verified among the common people; examined against the Three Kings without error; established before Heaven and Earth without contradiction; submitted to the spirits without doubt; and able to await a sage a hundred generations hence without perplexity. Submitted to the spirits without doubt shows he knows Heaven; awaiting a sage a hundred generations without perplexity shows he knows humanity — knowing Heaven and humanity is what steadies the Way. Therefore when the noble person moves, he becomes for generations the Way of all under Heaven; when he acts, the model; when he speaks, the standard. Those far look up with longing; those near never tire of him. The Odes' \"There, he is disliked by none; here, none grow weary of him; from dawn to night he strives, that his praise may endure forever\" shows that never has a noble person early won praise throughout the world without acting thus. The whole chapter lands a trustworthy way on layered testing — self, common people, the Three Kings, Heaven and Earth, the spirits, and sages a hundred generations hence."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `想讓自己的理念或做法被人信服，光靠說得好聽是不夠的，還需要禁得起檢驗——對照過去的智慧看是否站得住腳，讓身邊的人實際感受得到成效。經得起層層檢驗的信念，才能真正長久立足。
<br /><b>建議：今天在推廣一個想法或做法時，主動尋求他人的檢驗與回饋，而不是只顧自己覺得對。</b>`,
      en: `To make your ideas or methods genuinely convincing, sounding good isn't enough — they need to withstand scrutiny: holding up against past wisdom, and producing results the people around you can actually feel. Only convictions that survive layers of testing can truly stand the test of time.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, when promoting an idea or approach, actively seek out others' scrutiny and feedback rather than relying solely on your own sense that you're right.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 34,
    label: "30",
    text: {
      zh: `仲尼祖述堯舜，憲章文武；上律天時，下襲水土。辟如天地之無不持載，無不覆幬，辟如四時之錯行，如日月之代明。萬物並育而不相害，道並行而不相悖，小德川流，大德敦化，此天地之所以為大也。`,
      en: `Confucius handed down the traditions of Yao and Shun as if they were his ancestors, and took as his model the statutes of Wen and Wu. Above, he followed the order of Heaven's seasons; below, he conformed to the pattern of water and land. He is like Heaven and Earth, in that there is nothing they do not sustain, nothing they do not cover; he is like the four seasons in their alternating succession, like the sun and moon in their alternating brightness. All things are nourished together without harming one another; the Way proceeds side by side without contradiction. Lesser virtues flow like rivers and streams; great virtue is deep and transforming — this is what makes Heaven and Earth so great.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: `本章讚頌「仲尼祖述堯舜，憲章文武」——孔子把堯舜的道統當作祖先一般傳述，把文王武王的典章制度當作法度來效法；上律天時，下襲水土——上遵循天時的運行，下順應水土的地理。「辟如天地之無不持載，無不覆幬」——他的德行就像天地一樣，沒有什麼不承載、沒有什麼不覆蓋；「辟如四時之錯行，如日月之代明」——又像四季交錯運行、日月交替照耀一樣井然有序。「萬物並育而不相害，道並行而不相悖」——萬物一起生長而互不妨害，各種道理同時並行而互不衝突。「小德川流，大德敦化」——小的德行如江河細流分別流動，大的德行則渾厚敦實、化育萬物；此天地之所以為大也——這正是天地之所以偉大的原因。整章以孔子承述堯舜、憲章文武為起點，推到如天地持載覆幬、如四時日月錯行代明，再落到萬物並育、道並行、小德川流而大德敦化，說明聖人之大，正在能容能化，而不在排他獨尊。`,
      en: `This chapter praises Confucius: he handed down the traditions of Yao and Shun as if they were his ancestors, and took as his model the statutes of Wen and Wu. Above, he followed the order of Heaven's seasons; below, he conformed to the pattern of water and land. He is like Heaven and Earth, in that there is nothing they do not sustain, nothing they do not cover; he is like the four seasons in their alternating succession, like the sun and moon in their alternating brightness. All things are nourished together without harming one another; the Way proceeds side by side without contradiction. Lesser virtues flow like rivers and streams; great virtue is deep and transforming — this is what makes Heaven and Earth so great. The whole chapter begins from Confucius transmitting Yao and Shun and modeling Wen and Wu, presses on to sustaining and covering like Heaven and Earth and alternating like seasons and sun and moon, then lands on all things growing together, ways proceeding side by side, lesser virtues flowing and great virtue deeply transforming — showing that a sage's greatness lies in capacity to hold and transform, not in excluding all other ways.`
    },
    application: {
      zh: `真正宏大的格局，不是排除異己、獨尊一家，而是能包容萬物並存、各自發展而互不妨礙——就像天地讓萬物並育、四季各安其序。與人相處或帶領團隊時，追求的不該是整齊劃一，而是讓不同的人都能各展所長。
<br /><b>建議：今天在團隊或家庭中，留意是否有人的特質被你忽略或壓抑，試著給他多一點發展的空間。</b>`,
      en: `A truly great vision isn't about excluding those who differ or insisting on a single way — it's about allowing many things to coexist and develop side by side without interference, the way Heaven and Earth let all things grow together and the four seasons keep their own order. When working with people or leading a team, the goal shouldn't be uniformity, but letting different people each bring out their own strengths.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, notice whether someone's particular strength in your team or family has been overlooked or suppressed, and try to give them a bit more room to develop it.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 35,
    label: "31",
    text: {
      zh: `唯天下至聖，為能聰明睿知，足以有臨也；寬裕溫柔，足以有容也；發強剛毅，足以有執也；齊莊中正，足以有敬也；文理密察，足以有別也。溥博淵泉，而時出之。溥博如天，淵泉如淵。見而民莫不敬，言而民莫不信，行而民莫不說。是以聲名洋溢乎中國，施及蠻貊；舟車所至，人力所通；天之所覆，地之所載，日月所照，霜露所隊；凡有血氣者，莫不尊親，故曰配天。`,
      en: `Only the one who is utterly sagely in all under Heaven can have the acute hearing, clear sight, wisdom, and understanding needed to rule over others; can have breadth, generosity, warmth, and gentleness needed to embrace others; can have vigor, strength, firmness, and resolution needed to hold fast; can have gravity, correctness, centeredness, and uprightness needed to command respect; can have refinement, order, precision, and discernment needed to distinguish things clearly. Broad and expansive as Heaven, deep and unfathomable as an abyss, he manifests himself as the occasion requires. Broad and expansive like Heaven, deep and unfathomable like an abyss — when he appears, the people all revere him; when he speaks, the people all trust him; when he acts, the people all rejoice in him. Therefore his fame overflows and spreads throughout the Middle Kingdom, extending even to barbarian tribes; wherever ships and carriages reach, wherever human strength penetrates, wherever Heaven covers and Earth supports, wherever the sun and moon shine, wherever frost and dew fall — all who have blood and breath honor and love him. Therefore it is said that he is a match for Heaven.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章描繪「唯天下至聖」所能具備的五種力量，再推到聲名遠播、德配於天。唯天下至聖，為能聰明睿知，足以有臨也——耳目心思聰敏睿智，足以臨蒞在上；寬裕溫柔，足以有容也——胸懷寬廣、氣度溫柔，足以包容萬物；發強剛毅，足以有執也——奮發強健、剛強堅毅，足以執守不搖；齊莊中正，足以有敬也——整齊莊重、守中持正，足以使人起敬；文理密察，足以有別也——文采條理細密明察，足以辨析分別。這五種能力分別對應臨蒞、包容、執守、致敬、明辨，缺一便不足以稱至聖。這樣的聖人溥博淵泉，而時出之——德澤廣博如深泉，又能按時際自然流露出來：溥博如天，淵泉如淵——廣博像天，深沉像淵。見而民莫不敬，言而民莫不信，行而民莫不說——人們看見他無不敬、聽他說話無不信、看他行事無不喜悅。是以聲名洋溢乎中國，施及蠻貊——聲名充滿中原，並延及邊遠部族；舟車所至，人力所通；天之所覆，地之所載，日月所照，霜露所隊——凡是船車能到、人力能通、天所覆蓋、地所承載、日月所照、霜露所降之處；凡有血氣者，莫不尊親——一切有血氣的生命，無不尊敬親愛他。故曰配天——所以說他的德可以與天相配。整章把至聖之德，從五種內在能力推到聲名遠播、德配於天的外在效應。",
      en: "This chapter portrays what \"only the one who is utterly sagely under Heaven\" can possess in five powers, then presses on to fame spreading afar and virtue matching Heaven. Only the utterly sagely can be acute in hearing, clear in sight, wise and understanding, enough to rule over others; broad, generous, warm, and gentle, enough to embrace; vigorous, strong, firm, and resolute, enough to hold fast; grave, correct, centered, and upright, enough to command respect; refined, orderly, precise, and discerning, enough to distinguish clearly — answering to ruling, embracing, holding, reverence, and discernment. Missing any one of these five is not enough to be called utterly sagely. Such a sage is broad and expansive as a deep spring, issuing forth as the occasion requires: broad like Heaven, deep like an abyss. When he appears, the people all revere him; when he speaks, all trust him; when he acts, all rejoice. Therefore his fame overflows the Middle Kingdom and reaches even distant tribes; wherever ships and carriages reach, human strength penetrates, Heaven covers, Earth supports, sun and moon shine, frost and dew fall — all who have blood and breath honor and love him. Hence it is said he is a match for Heaven. The whole chapter moves from five inner capacities of utmost sageliness to the outer effect of fame spreading afar and virtue matching Heaven."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `真正令人信服的領導或影響力，不是單靠某一項特質（例如口才或魄力），而是多種特質的均衡發展——既要有智慧與判斷力，也要有包容心，還要有原則與堅持。全面均衡的修養，才能自然而然贏得他人的敬重與信任。
<br /><b>建議：今天檢視自己在智慧、包容、堅持、莊重、明辨這五方面，哪一項最欠缺，並有意識地加強它。</b>`,
      en: `Truly convincing leadership or influence doesn't rest on just one trait, like eloquence or force of will — it comes from the balanced development of many qualities: wisdom and judgment, but also a capacity for acceptance, along with firm principle and resolve. Well-rounded, balanced cultivation is what naturally earns others' respect and trust.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, examine which of these five qualities — wisdom, acceptance, resolve, gravity, and discernment — you're most lacking in, and consciously work to strengthen it.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 36,
    label: "32",
    text: {
      zh: `唯天下至誠，為能經綸天下之大經，立天下之大本，知天地之化育。夫焉有所倚？肫肫其仁！淵淵其淵！浩浩其天！苟不固聰明聖知達天德者，其孰能知之？`,
      en: `Only the one who is utterly sincere in all under Heaven can weave together the great web of relations for all under Heaven, establish the great root of all under Heaven, and understand the transforming and nourishing work of Heaven and Earth. On what does he depend? How earnest and sincere is his humaneness! How deep and unfathomable is his depth! How vast and boundless is his Heaven! Truly, unless one is one who is firmly endowed with acute hearing, clear sight, sageliness, and wisdom, penetrating to the virtue of Heaven, who could know him?`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章再次歌詠「至誠」的極致境界：唯有天下至誠之人，才能理順天下人倫的重大綱紀，樹立天下的根本，並且理解天地化育萬物的奧祕。他這樣的成就並非依靠外在憑藉，而是源自內心懇切篤厚的仁德、深邃難測的心境，以及浩瀚廣大的胸懷，就像天一樣。若不是本身就具備聰明睿智、通達天德的人，實在難以真正理解這樣的至誠之人。",
      en: "This chapter once more celebrates the highest state of \"utmost sincerity\": only the person who is utterly sincere in all under Heaven can weave together the great fabric of human relationships, establish the great root of the world, and understand the mystery of Heaven and Earth's work of transformation and nourishment. Such achievement rests on nothing external, but flows from an earnest and deeply humane heart, a mind as unfathomably deep as an abyss, and a spirit as vast and boundless as Heaven itself. Only one who is truly endowed with acute perception, sageliness, and wisdom penetrating to the very virtue of Heaven could ever fully understand such a person of utmost sincerity."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `真正深厚的人格與能力，往往難以被表面短暫的相處所看穿——它需要時間與同等的深度才能真正理解與欣賞。不必急著讓所有人都認可你內在的真誠與深度，持續涵養自己，懂的人自然會懂。
<br /><b>建議：今天不必急於證明自己的深度或誠意給每個人看，專注涵養內在，時間會證明一切。</b>`,
      en: `Truly deep character and ability often can't be seen through in a brief, surface-level encounter — it takes time, and comparable depth in the other person, to really understand and appreciate it. There's no need to rush to have everyone recognize your inner sincerity and depth; keep cultivating yourself, and those who can perceive it eventually will.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, don't rush to prove your depth or sincerity to everyone — focus on cultivating your inner self, and let time reveal the rest.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 37,
    label: "33-1",
    text: {
      zh: `《詩》曰「衣錦尚絅」，惡其文之著也。故君子之道，闇然而日章；小人之道，的然而日亡。君子之道：淡而不厭，簡而文，溫而理，知遠之近，知風之自，知微之顯，可與入德矣。`,
      en: `The Book of Odes says, "She wears a brocade robe, yet covers it with a plain outer garment" — because she dislikes ostentatious display. Thus the way of the noble person appears hidden and unassuming, yet grows daily more illustrious; the way of the petty person appears bright and conspicuous, yet fades daily into nothing. The way of the noble person is bland yet never wearisome, simple yet refined, gentle yet ordered; he knows that the far begins in the near, knows the source from which influence spreads, and knows that the subtle becomes manifest — such a one may enter into virtue.`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "《詩經》說「身穿錦繡衣裳，外面卻罩上一件素淡的罩衫」，是因為厭惡過於張揚顯露。因此君子之道，看似隱晦不顯，卻日漸彰顯光大；小人之道，看似鮮明張揚，卻日漸消亡。君子的修養，平淡卻讓人不生厭倦，簡約卻有文采，溫和卻條理分明；他明白深遠的道理其實從切近之處做起，明白外在的影響其實源自內心，也明白細微之處終將顯現出來——懂得這些，就可以說是進入了德行的門檻。",
      en: "The Book of Odes says, \"She wears a brocade robe, yet covers it with a plain outer garment\" — because she dislikes ostentatious display. Thus the way of the noble person appears hidden and unassuming at first, yet grows daily more illustrious, while the way of the petty person appears bright and showy, yet fades daily into nothing. The noble person's cultivation is bland yet never tiresome, simple yet refined, gentle yet well ordered; he understands that what is far off begins in what is near, that outward influence is rooted within, and that what is subtle will eventually become manifest — grasping these things, one may be said to have entered upon virtue."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `真正扎實的修養或才能，不需要靠外在的張揚來證明，反而是愈低調內斂，愈能日久見真章；相反地，急於表現、鋒芒畢露的人，往往後繼無力，很快就黯淡下去。
<br /><b>建議：今天做一件好事或展現一項能力時，試著不刻意張揚，讓成果自己說話。</b>`,
      en: `Genuine, substantial cultivation or ability doesn't need to be proven through outward display — in fact, the more understated and restrained it is, the more it proves itself over time. By contrast, those eager to show off and display their brilliance often run out of substance and fade quickly.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, when you do something good or demonstrate an ability, try not to make a show of it — let the results speak for themselves.</b>`
    }
  },
  {
    id: 38,
    label: "33-2",
    text: {
      zh: `《詩》云：「潛雖伏矣，亦孔之昭！」故君子內省不疚，無惡於志。君子之所不可及者，其唯人之所不見乎。《詩》云：「相在爾室，尚不愧於屋漏。」故君子不動而敬，不言而信。《詩》曰：「奏假無言，時靡有爭。」是故君子不賞而民勸，不怒而民威於鈇鉞。《詩》曰：「不顯惟德！百辟其刑之。」是故君子篤恭而天下平。《詩》云：「予懷明德，不大聲以色。」子曰：「聲色之於以化民，末也。」《詩》曰：「德輶如毛」，毛猶有倫。「上天之載，無聲無臭」，至矣！`,
      en: `The Book of Odes says, "Although it lies hidden and submerged, it is yet greatly manifest!" Therefore the noble person examines himself inwardly and finds no cause for shame, no evil in his purposes. That wherein the noble person is unmatched by others lies precisely in what others cannot see. The Book of Odes says, "Even when alone in your own room, you should have no cause to be ashamed before the light that enters through the northwest corner." Therefore the noble person is reverent even when he does not move, and trustworthy even when he does not speak. The Book of Odes says, "He advances to the sacrifice in reverent silence; at that time no one contends." Therefore the noble person, without offering rewards, yet the people are encouraged to virtue; without displaying anger, yet the people are more awed than by battle-axes. The Book of Odes says, "He makes no display of his virtue, yet all the princes pattern themselves upon it." Therefore the noble person, being sincerely reverent and humble, brings peace to all under Heaven. The Book of Odes says, "I cherish your bright virtue, which makes no great sound or outward show." The Master said, "In transforming the people, sound and outward show are of the least importance." The Book of Odes says, "Virtue is as light as a hair" — yet even a hair still has some comparable weight. "The workings of High Heaven have neither sound nor smell" — that is perfection!`
    },
    plain: {
      zh: "本章連引多章《詩經》，層層推到君子修養的極致——由人所不見之處的慎獨，一路推到無聲無臭。先引「潛雖伏矣，亦孔之昭」——即使潛藏隱伏，依然極其昭著；故君子內省不疚，無惡於志——向內省察不覺內疚，志向之中沒有惡念；君子之所不可及者，其唯人之所不見乎——別人所趕不上他的地方，正是在別人看不見之處。再引「相在爾室，尚不愧於屋漏」——即使獨處室中，面對屋漏（室內西北角）透進的光也不愧疚；故君子不動而敬，不言而信——不必有動作已自有恭敬，不必說話已自有信實。又引「奏假無言，時靡有爭」——祭祀進獻時肅穆無言、當時無人爭競；故君子不賞而民勸，不怒而民威於鈇鉞——不必賞賜百姓已自相勸勉，不必發怒百姓已畏服超過斧鉞之刑。又引「不顯惟德！百辟其刑之」——德不必刻意張揚顯赫，諸侯都來效法；故君子篤恭而天下平——君子篤實恭敬，天下便平治。再引「予懷明德，不大聲以色」，孔子評曰：聲色之於以化民，末也——靠聲調神色去教化人民，只是末節。最後以「德輶如毛」猶有倫可比——說德輕如毛髮，畢竟還有可類比的分量；而「上天之載，無聲無臭」為至——上天的運行無聲無氣味，這才是極致。最高之德如天運，無聲無臭而無所不成。整章由慎獨、無愧，推到不賞而勸、篤恭而天下平，終以無聲無臭為修養極致。",
      en: "This chapter strings together several Odes, pressing step by step to the utmost of the noble person's cultivation — from watchfulness in what others cannot see all the way to the soundless and scentless. First: \"Although it lies hidden and submerged, it is yet greatly manifest\" — even hidden, it is still utterly clear; therefore the noble person examines himself inwardly and finds no cause for shame, no evil in his purposes, and that wherein he is unmatched lies precisely in what others cannot see. Next: \"Even when alone in your own room, you should have no cause to be ashamed before the light that enters through the northwest corner\" — therefore he is reverent even when he does not move, trustworthy even when he does not speak. Then: \"He advances to the sacrifice in reverent silence; at that time no one contends\" — therefore without rewards the people are encouraged, without anger they are more awed than by battle-axes. Then: \"He makes no display of his virtue, yet all the princes pattern themselves upon it\" — therefore, being sincerely reverent and humble, he brings peace to all under Heaven. Then: \"I cherish your bright virtue, which makes no great sound or outward show,\" and Confucius adds that in transforming the people, sound and outward show are of the least importance. Finally, \"Virtue is as light as a hair\" still has some comparable weight, while \"the workings of High Heaven have neither sound nor smell\" is perfection — the highest virtue is like Heaven's working, soundless and scentless yet accomplishing everywhere. The whole chapter moves from watchfulness in solitude and freedom from shame, through encouraging without rewards and pacifying the world through sincere reverence, to soundless, scentless Heaven as cultivation's utmost."
    },
    application: {
      zh: `修養的最高境界，不是靠外在的獎懲、聲勢或表演來影響他人，而是內心真誠篤實到一個地步，自然而然感召周遭的人。愈是安靜、樸實、毫不張揚的存在，往往愈能發揮深遠的影響力。
<br /><b>建議：今天嘗試不靠言語或獎懲去影響一個人，而是單純用你真誠一致的行為去感染他。</b>`,
      en: `The highest state of cultivation doesn't influence others through external rewards, punishments, or displays — it comes from an inner sincerity so substantial that it naturally moves the people around you. The quieter, plainer, and less ostentatious a presence, the more far-reaching its influence often turns out to be.
<br /><b>Suggestion: Today, try influencing someone not through words or incentives, but simply through the quiet consistency of your sincere behavior.</b>`
    }
  }
];

export default doctrineOfMean2;
